Z||000004||batboy||05-15-2000||12:14 AM||||If I remember correctly, mid-80's to about 1990 or so Beretta or Grand Am. I used 88 Grand Am parts and had no problem. Uaana, all the references I saw and what I did was to grind a little off the spindle mounting pads where the caliper attaches. Grinding on the caliper is not advisable in my book. The article I used has been moved and I can't find the new URL. That was the Randy Agree write up.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Monkeyman||05-15-2000||10:05 AM||||Although that was the article I was looking for, I seem to remember another one which described (in great detail) exactly what to do and how to do the front and the rear using Beretta parts.  BB, was that the one you were talking about?  I can (don't have any choice) wait to do the front, but the rear just seems so easy.  BB, what did you have to grind off, how much and why?||||reg||1
